
Que:
- add flag to check if changes were made before saving 
- core.py/shell.py mix `from src.configs import ...` with bare `from configs import ...` /
    `from utils import ...` (lazy imports inside methods). The bare form only resolves because
    setup.sh launches `python -m que.shell`/`que.server` from inside src/; it breaks under pytest
    from the repo root (tests/que/test_shell.py's TestSetMaxRuns has to prepend src/ to sys.path),
    and can load the same module twice under two names. Switch them all to `src.`-prefixed imports.

Shell:
- add more flexibility to run summarisation
- add a flag to turn into debugging mode. Check debugging mode in unwrap_exception
- fix argument parsing short flags
- nargs="+" options swallow following positionals: e.g. `edit to_run 0 -ek acc 0.99` fails
    with "required: value" because -ek/--edit_keys consumes 0.99; the value has to be written
    before the flag (`edit to_run 0 0.99 -ek acc`). -c/--criterion has the same problem, so it
    must come last. Affects any parser mixing nargs="+" options with trailing positionals.
- indirect indexing is racy: edit/display/copy/to_config resolve the filtered/sorted index
    against a client-side snapshot (list_runs), then act on the server by original index in a
    separate call. If the queue changes in between (e.g. the worker pops to_run[0]), edit_run
    silently edits a different run (it re-validates fine, so nothing flags it). Possible fix:
    pass the expected run to edit_run and raise if peak_run(loc, idx) differs (note
    debug.any_dups -- content comparison only works if runs are unique).

Daemon:
- Add better checking when starting daemon/worker (check it is actually working instead of just reporting
    that the process started successfully)
- [DONE 2026-09-22] supervise()'s loop passed `dict(sweep).copy() if sweep is not None else None`
    to Worker.start() -- but `sweep` is a DictProxy fetched once before the loop, so it is never
    actually `None`, even when the underlying dict is empty. This meant an empty/no-op sweep
    ({}) was always passed through as a truthy-looking dict, so Worker.start() (which checks
    `sweep_info is not None`) would try to run a sweep trial with no sweep configured, hit
    sweep_info_validate()'s ValidationError, crash the worker subprocess, and (with the default
    stop_on_fail=True) stop the whole supervisor -- surfaced while adding the max_runs cap, whose
    stop mechanism (clearing the sweep once the cap is hit) triggers exactly this case. Fixed by
    checking the materialized dict's truthiness instead of the proxy handle's identity.


Worker:
- if GPU is busy on startup, gets stuck, needs server process to be restarted
- [DONE 2026-09-18] silent-swallow visibility gap in _sweep_train: wandb's hyperband/
    early-terminate stops a sweep trial by injecting a bare, message-less `Exception` into
    the training thread (ctypes.pythonapi.PyThreadState_SetAsyncExc, in wandb's own
    pyagent.py _terminate_thread), which wandb's own `_run_job` then swallows internally
    (calls wandb.finish(exit_code=1), doesn't re-raise) -- so it never reached
    Worker.sweep()'s except Exception, and the trial fell through to testing/old_runs with
    zero log trace that it had been cut short (this outcome is actually desired, since
    earlier-epoch checkpoints are still usable, but it was happening by accident). Fixed by
    _is_wandb_injected_stop() + a try/except around the train_loop() call in _sweep_train
    that detects this specific signature and logs it explicitly, instead of relying on
    wandb's internal swallow. Test: tests/que/test_worker.py.
- [DONE 2026-09-18] genuine crash during a sweep trial's train_loop() was ALSO invisible,
    not just the hyperband case above: wandb's own agent thread (pyagent.py's _run_job)
    swallows *any* exception from _sweep_train unconditionally, not just the injected
    hyperband one -- confirmed live on the debug sweep (a real shape-mismatch RuntimeError
    fell straight through to testing against an empty checkpoints dir). Worker.sweep()'s
    except Exception / stash_failed_run was dead code for this. Fixed by handling the
    non-wandb-stop branch directly inside _sweep_train's except block: stash to fail_runs +
    log there (mirrors Worker.train()'s existing pattern), which also empties cur_run so
    Worker.start()'s existing len_loc('cur_run')==1 dispatch correctly skips testing.
    Verified end-to-end with a forced-crash debug sweep trial (fail_runs gained the entry,
    cur_run emptied, test() correctly skipped).
- broader: train/sweep/test's outer `except Exception` blocks (worker.py's _fail path)
    still blindly stash/report every non-QueException the same way -- e.g. no distinction
    between a real crash and GPU OOM. Need to distinguish exception types explicitly instead
    of blanket `except Exception` + str(e) throughout worker.py/training.py. See also
    src/TODO.md's repo-wide blind-except audit item.

Server:
- wandb's own per-run "Logs" tab / local output.log has been empty for a long time --
    NOT a block-scoping bug in the `redirect_stdout(self.log_adapter)` calls in
    _train/_sweep_train (tried merging the setup/training blocks into one continuous
    redirect on 2026-09-18; verified on a live debug-sweep trial that this made no
    difference -- output.log stayed empty, reverted). Real mechanism: wandb's
    wandb/sdk/lib/console_capture.py patches the *write method* of whichever object is
    sys.stdout at the first `import wandb` in the process, once, permanently ("not
    possible to unpatch or repatch" per its own docstring). Our redirect_stdout(...)
    reassigns the sys.stdout *name* to self.log_adapter during training, so print()
    calls self.log_adapter.write() directly and never touch wandb's patched object at
    all -- no restructuring of our `with` blocks changes that. Real fix needs to either
    (a) not reassign sys.stdout during training at all (which likely means moving
    training.py's print()-based step/epoch logging to a real logger, removing the need
    for redirect_stdout here in the first place -- training.py's bare print() calls are
    the underlying quick-and-dirty pattern), or (b) additionally register
    self.log_adapter's write via wandb.sdk.lib.console_capture.capture_stdout(...)
    directly so both wandb's capture and our own log file see the same writes (couples
    us to a wandb-internal module path, so needs a version-pin/compat note if taken).
- redirect testing output to log file


